# SRG-APP-000122-AU-000140  -  CAT II
# The Central Log Server must protect audit tools from unauthorized
# modification.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000122-AU-000140
group:           V-221918
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-001494
control_rev5:    AU-9

contribution:    The application gates modification of its audit tools and audit settings
                 behind two capabilities it ships to the administrator role only, so its
                 share is that neither reaches a general-purpose role.

governs:         authorize.conf - Used to create roles.

check:           authorize.conf [role_user] admin_all_objects absent
check:           authorize.conf [role_power] admin_all_objects absent
check:           authorize.conf [role_user] change_audit absent
check:           authorize.conf [role_power] change_audit absent

external:        Write permissions on the Splunk binaries and application directories -
                 SPLK-CL-000190 implements this at the operating system layer
external:        Object-level write access recorded in default.meta, which the btool-based
                 collector does not read

citation:        restmap.conf capability.post present and capability.put present are
                 removed for the reason given on SRG-APP-000121-AU-000130: the keys are
                 real (restmap.conf.spec:111-112, 303-312) but the stanza exists only where
                 an app declares its own endpoint, so the conditions fail a conformant
                 deployment that declares none. admin_all_objects absent read over
                 [role_<roleName>] fails every install, because the shipped role_admin
                 holds it and must (authorize.conf:290); pinning it to the general-purpose
                 roles keeps the test and removes the false finding. change_audit is added
                 because it is the product's own named gate on changing audit settings and,
                 like admin_all_objects, ships to role_admin alone, so the condition passes
                 stock and fails a genuine grant.
finding:         A general-purpose role holds admin_all_objects or change_audit, so the
                 audit tools or the audit configuration can be modified by an account that
                 is not privileged.
